::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve the system y\=x+3 and y\=7.
{
=A. x\=4 #Correct. Substitute y\=7 into 7\=x+3, then subtract 3.
~B. x\=3 #This uses the constant from the equation, not the solution.
~C. x\=7 #This is the y-value, not the x-value.
~D. x\=10 #This adds 7 and 3 instead of solving.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
Which point satisfies both y>2x and y<9?
{
~A. (4,8) #This gives y\=8, but 8 is not greater than 2(4)\=8.
=B. (3,7) #Correct. 7>6 and 7<9, so both inequalities are true.
~C. (5,11) #This is greater than 9, so it fails the second inequality.
~D. (2,3) #This is less than 2(2)\=4, so it fails the first inequality.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.4
Question:
A line and a parabola intersect at (-1,2) and (3,6). What are the system solutions?
{
~A. x\=2 and x\=6 #These are y-values, not complete system solution inputs.
~B. x\=-1 only #This misses the second intersection.
=C. (-1,2) and (3,6) #Correct. System solutions are the points that satisfy both equations.
~D. No solution #Two intersection points are given, so the system has solutions.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6
Question:
How does y\=(x-4)²+1 transform y\=x²?
{
~A. Left 4 and up 1 #The inside sign is interpreted oppositely.
~B. Right 4 and down 1 #The outside constant is positive, so the shift is up.
~C. Left 1 and up 4 #This switches the horizontal and vertical shifts.
=D. Right 4 and up 1 #Correct. x-4 shifts right 4, and +1 shifts up 1.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)\=x+2 and g(x)\=5x, what is f(g(2))?
{
=A. 12 #Correct. g(2)\=10, then f(10)\=12.
~B. 10 #This stops after evaluating g(2).
~C. 7 #This applies f to 5 instead of applying g to 2 first.
~D. 20 #This multiplies after adding instead of composing.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
For (f/g)(x), which inputs must be excluded?
{
~A. Inputs where f(x)\=0 #A zero numerator is allowed in a quotient.
=B. Inputs where g(x)\=0 #Correct. The denominator cannot equal zero.
~C. All negative inputs #Negative values are not automatically excluded.
~D. All real numbers #Only inputs that make the denominator zero are excluded.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Find the inverse of f(x)\=2x-6.
{
~A. f⁻¹(x)\=2x+6 #This does not reverse the order of operations.
~B. f⁻¹(x)\=(x-6)/2 #The sign should change when undoing -6.
=C. f⁻¹(x)\=(x+6)/2 #Correct. Add 6 first, then divide by 2.
~D. f⁻¹(x)\=1/(2x-6) #Inverse function notation does not mean reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10
Question:
Which check verifies that f and g are inverse functions?
{
~A. f(x)+g(x)\=0 #This checks additive opposites, not inverse functions.
~B. f(x)g(x)\=1 #This checks reciprocals in special cases, not inverse functions.
~C. f(x)\=g(x) #Inverse functions do not usually have identical rules.
=D. f(g(x))\=x and g(f(x))\=x #Correct. Both compositions must return the original input.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
What is the average rate of change from (1,5) to (4,17)?
{
=A. 4 #Correct. (17-5)/(4-1)\=12/3\=4.
~B. 3 #This is the change in input, not the rate.
~C. 12 #This is the change in output before dividing.
~D. 22 #This adds the output values.
}

::ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_Pretest_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
A context gives possible times t\=-3 and t\=8. Which value is viable?
{
~A. t\=-3 only #Negative time before the starting point is not viable.
=B. t\=8 only #Correct. The positive time value is viable in this context.
~C. Both values #The negative time value violates the context.
~D. Neither value #The positive time value is viable.
}

